=== L1 Design Strategies ===
id: ef6c1523-ba20-46c9-9af8-41841772f98f
slug: lesson-01
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:56:05.487354+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Six design strategies — linear, iterative, inclusive, user-centred, sustainable and ergonomic — their contexts.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Design Strategy?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A structured approach that guides how a designer or engineering team tackles a design problem from brief to final product.">design strategy</dfn> is the overarching approach a designer chooses before any sketching or modelling begins. The strategy shapes every decision that follows: how much time is spent on research, whether users are consulted, and how many times the design is revised. You need to know six named strategies, understand when each suits a project, and weigh up the advantages and disadvantages of each.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Linear Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="A design strategy that moves through a fixed sequence of stages — brief, research, specification, design, make, evaluate — without returning to earlier stages.">Linear design</dfn> follows a step-by-step sequence: analyse the brief, research, write a specification, generate ideas, make a prototype, then evaluate. Each stage is completed before the next begins, and the process does not loop back. This approach suits projects with a very clear, stable brief — for example, manufacturing a replacement component to an agreed technical standard where the requirements are already fully known.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The main advantage of linear design is its predictability: timelines and budgets are easier to plan because the scope is fixed. The disadvantage is inflexibility — if a problem is discovered late in the process (say, during manufacture) there is no formal mechanism to revise the design, which can be costly.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover the box and list all six design strategies from memory. For each one, write one context where it suits the project best.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six design strategies you must know are: linear, iterative, inclusive, user-centred, sustainable, and ergonomic design. Questions may ask you to recommend one strategy for a given context and justify your choice — always name the strategy precisely and then explain why it fits that scenario.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Iterative Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="A cyclical design strategy in which the designer repeatedly designs, makes, evaluates and improves a product through successive cycles until requirements are met.">Iterative design</dfn> is a cyclic process: the designer produces an idea, tests or evaluates it, identifies weaknesses, then redesigns and tests again. Each loop refines the product. This strategy suits complex or novel products where requirements are not fully understood at the start — consumer electronics, medical devices and bespoke engineering solutions all benefit from an iterative approach.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The advantage of iterative design is that problems are caught early and the product is progressively improved to better meet the brief. The disadvantage is that the process can be time-consuming and expensive if many revision cycles are needed, and it requires clear decision criteria for when to stop iterating.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Inclusive Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Inclusive design aims to create products that can be used by the widest possible range of people, regardless of age, ability or background — without the need for specialist adaptation. A classic engineering example is the OXO Good Grips peeler, designed so that people with limited grip strength (including those with arthritis) can use it as easily as anyone else. Inclusive design suits products that will be used in public spaces or by diverse populations.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The advantage is that an inclusive product reaches a larger market and avoids the need for costly specialist versions. The disadvantage is that designing for the full range of users can add complexity and cost to the development process, and may mean compromising on optimisation for any one group.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>User-centred design: keeping the user at the heart of every decision</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">User-centred design (UCD) places the needs, preferences and limitations of the end user at the centre of every design decision. Designers gather detailed user research — through interviews, observation and testing — and continually involve users throughout the process. UCD suits consumer products where user experience is critical, such as smartphone interfaces, hospital equipment or workplace tools. The advantage is that the final product is highly likely to meet real user needs, reducing the chance of market failure. The disadvantage is that extensive user research and testing adds time and cost, and user feedback can sometimes conflict or be difficult to interpret.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Sustainable Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Sustainable design considers the environmental impact of a product across its entire lifecycle — from raw material extraction through manufacture, use and eventual disposal. Designers using this strategy might choose recycled materials, minimise energy use in manufacture, or design for disassembly so components can be reused. It suits any product where environmental credentials are a key market requirement — from packaging to renewable energy hardware.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The advantage of sustainable design is reduced environmental impact and, increasingly, commercial appeal as consumers prioritise green credentials. The disadvantage is that sustainable materials and processes can cost more, and life-cycle analysis adds complexity to the design process.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="For each strategy, practise writing one advantage AND one disadvantage in a single sentence each — exam questions often award marks for a paired advantage/disadvantage comparison.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Linear design is the only strategy that does not include a formal feedback loop — all other strategies involve some form of review or refinement. This distinction is important: exam questions frequently ask you to explain why a company chose an iterative rather than a linear approach, or vice versa.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Ergonomic Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Ergonomic design optimises the interaction between a product and the human body — considering posture, reach, force, comfort and safety. Engineers use <dfn class="term" data-def="Statistical data about the dimensions of the human body, used to ensure products fit the range of people who will use them.">anthropometric data</dfn> (measurements of human body dimensions) to ensure products fit the range of users. Ergonomic design is essential for tools, workstations, vehicles and any equipment used repeatedly over long periods, where poor fit leads to injury or error.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">The advantage of ergonomic design is improved user safety and comfort, which reduces injury rates and increases productivity. The disadvantage is that designing for the full range of body sizes requires detailed anthropometric research, and it may not always be possible to satisfy every user simultaneously — some products are designed for the 5th to 95th percentile range, which still excludes a small proportion of users.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Choosing the right strategy: matching approach to context</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n21">Exam questions will often give you a design scenario and ask which strategy would be most appropriate. Use this logic: if the brief is fixed and the product is a known type (e.g. replacing a standard bracket), linear design is efficient. If the product is novel or complex, iterative design allows refinement. If the product must serve diverse or less-abled users, consider inclusive or user-centred design. If the client specifically requires low environmental impact, sustainable design is the priority. If the product is worn or handled repeatedly and must fit the human body well, ergonomic design takes precedence. A strong answer will name the strategy, describe its key feature, and link it directly to the scenario — never just list strategies without explanation.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The six design strategies are linear, iterative, inclusive, user-centred, sustainable and ergonomic — each suits a different context, and you must know when to recommend each one and be able to state at least one advantage and one disadvantage of each.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Linear design is the only strategy without a feedback loop; iterative design is explicitly cyclic and is used when requirements evolve during development.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Ergonomic design relies on anthropometric data to match products to the human body, while inclusive design broadens usability for the widest possible range of people — these two strategies are related but distinct.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">When a question asks you to "recommend a design strategy" for a scenario, name the strategy precisely (use the exact term from the spec) and then justify your choice with at least two reasons linked to the scenario. Vague answers such as "they should use a good strategy" score zero. For "advantages and disadvantages" questions, give a specific advantage and a specific disadvantage — do not simply state that one strategy is "better" than another without explaining why in context.</p>

