=== L2 The Iterative Design Process ===
id: 932ec89c-65d5-47d3-9610-14715886a0b9
slug: lesson-02
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:41:48.460709+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The stages of the iterative (cyclic) design process — design, make and evaluate — and the activities carried out.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">A Cyclic Approach to Design</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The iterative design process is the cyclic, step-by-step framework through which most engineering design projects progress. Unlike linear design, the process loops back: after evaluating a prototype, the designer returns to the design stage to improve it, then makes and evaluates again. The iterative process is structured into two main stages — <dfn class="term" data-def="The first main stage of the iterative design process, covering analysis of the brief, research, specification writing, and generation of design ideas.">Design</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="The second main stage of the iterative design process, covering modelling, prototype manufacture and evaluation against the brief and specification.">Make and Evaluate</dfn> — each containing several distinct activities that you must be able to describe.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Stage 1 — Design: Analysing the Brief</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Every iterative cycle begins with a thorough analysis of the <dfn class="term" data-def="A document issued by a client that outlines the problem to be solved, the intended user, and any constraints such as budget or materials.">design brief</dfn>. The designer reads the brief carefully to identify what the product must do, who will use it, any constraints (cost, size, materials), and what success looks like. This analysis informs every later decision — if the brief is misunderstood, the entire cycle produces a product that fails to meet the client's needs.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Stage 1 — Design: Researching Product Requirements</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Once the brief is understood, the designer researches what the product must achieve. Research falls into two types. <dfn class="term" data-def="Research that gathers new, first-hand information directly from users or experiments, such as interviews, surveys, focus groups and user observations.">Primary research</dfn> gathers first-hand information from direct sources — interviews with potential users, focus groups, user observations, and the use of tables of anthropometric data to understand body dimensions. The advantage of primary research is that it is specific to the product; the disadvantage is that it is time-consuming and expensive to gather.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Secondary research uses existing published information — books, design guides, technical standards, and market research reports. Analysing existing products is a key secondary research activity: the ACCESS FM framework (Aesthetics, Cost, Customer, Environment, Size, Safety, Function, Materials and Manufacturing) provides a structured checklist for evaluating a competitor or similar product. Product disassembly — physically taking apart an existing product — is a secondary research technique that reveals how it is constructed and what materials are used. Secondary research is faster and cheaper than primary research but may be out of date or not specific to the new product.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n8" data-revision-tip="Cover this box and write from memory the nine headings of ACCESS FM. Then, for each heading, give one question a designer would ask when analysing an existing product.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>ACCESS FM stands for Aesthetics, Cost, Customer, Environment, Size, Safety, Function, Materials and Manufacturing. This framework is used both as a research tool (to analyse existing products) and as a structure for writing the design specification — the same nine criteria appear as the types of criteria included in a specification.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Stage 1 — Design: The Engineering Design Specification</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">After research, the designer writes an <dfn class="term" data-def="A detailed document that sets out the measurable criteria a product must meet, derived from the design brief and research; used to guide design decisions and evaluate outcomes.">engineering design specification</dfn>. The specification converts the client's needs into precise, testable criteria — for example, specifying that a product must weigh no more than 500 g, withstand a force of 200 N, or be produced for under £15. The specification is used throughout the iterative cycle to check whether the design is progressing in the right direction.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Stage 1 — Design: Generating Design Ideas</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">With a specification in place, the designer generates ideas. At the start of the first cycle, generation means creating new concepts through sketching (freehand drawings showing proportions, features and materials) and modelling (physical mock-ups or virtual 3D models). In later iterations, generation refers to the modification or improvement of an existing design in response to evaluation feedback. The ability to move fluidly between sketching and modelling — and between first ideas and refined revisions — is central to the iterative approach.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why both sketching and modelling matter in idea generation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Sketching is fast and cheap — a designer can explore ten different forms in the time it would take to build one physical model. It communicates concepts quickly to a client or colleague. However, sketching cannot reveal how a 3D object actually fits together or moves. Physical and virtual modelling fills this gap: a 3D CAD model allows the designer to check proportions, spot interference between components, and simulate function before any material is cut. Both techniques complement each other, and in a thorough iterative process, ideas move between sketch and model multiple times before becoming a prototype.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Stage 2 — Make and Evaluate: Modelling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The make-and-evaluate stage begins with modelling. Models are made before a final prototype to test specific aspects of the design without the full cost of manufacture. The reasons for modelling are to test proportions (does the product look and feel the right size?), to test scale (does it fit the intended space or the user's hand?), and to test function (does it move, connect or operate as intended?).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Modelling takes two forms. Virtual modelling uses CAD software to create a digital representation; this allows dimensions to be checked, components to be assembled on screen, and simulations of stress or movement to be run — all before any physical material is used. Physical modelling creates a tangible object from card, foam, clay or other cheap materials; this allows the designer to assess ergonomics, user interaction and aesthetics in a way that a screen image cannot replicate.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: three reasons for modelling (proportions, scale, function) and two types of modelling (virtual and physical). Practise giving one advantage of each type.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Modelling is carried out to test three things: proportions, scale and function. Virtual modelling uses CAD software; physical modelling uses materials such as card, foam or clay. The distinction between virtual and physical modelling — and the specific things each can test — is directly examinable.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Stage 2 — Make and Evaluate: Manufacture and Comparison</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">After modelling, the designer manufactures or modifies a prototype — a working version of the product built to check whether it meets the brief and specification. The prototype is then evaluated: its dimensions, function and quality are compared against the requirements of the design brief and specification. Where the prototype falls short, the cycle restarts — the designer returns to the design stage, modifies the idea, and makes a revised prototype. This loop continues until the product meets all specification criteria or until the project constraints (time or budget) require a final decision.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common mistake: confusing a model with a prototype</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">A model is made to test specific aspects of a design (proportions, scale, function) before committing to full manufacture — it is often not the same size, material or finish as the final product. A prototype is a working version of the product that is evaluated against the full design brief and specification. The distinction matters in exam questions: if asked about modelling, focus on testing and exploration; if asked about prototyping, focus on comparison against the specification and the decision to iterate or finalise. Mixing up these terms suggests a surface-level understanding and will cost marks on evaluation questions.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The iterative design process has two main stages — Design (brief analysis, research, specification, idea generation) and Make and Evaluate (modelling, prototype manufacture, comparison against the brief) — and the cycle repeats until the product meets all requirements.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Primary research gathers first-hand data from users (including anthropometric data, interviews and focus groups); secondary research uses existing sources including ACCESS FM analysis of existing products and product disassembly.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Modelling is carried out to test proportions, scale and function, using either virtual (CAD) or physical methods, before a prototype is manufactured and compared against the design specification.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">Questions on the iterative design process often ask you to describe what happens at a particular stage — always include both the activity and its purpose. For example, do not just write "the designer does research"; write "the designer carries out primary research by interviewing potential users to gather first-hand data about the product's requirements, which is then used to write the design specification." Linking each activity to its outcome and to the next stage is what separates Secure from Mastering answers.</p>

