=== L3 Design Requirements and Specifications ===
id: 422b43e1-b77d-47e3-89b0-417923319242
slug: lesson-03
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:29:43.755992+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Design requirements, the difference between needs and wants, quantitative and qualitative criteria, and writing a.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Design Requirements?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before any design work begins, an engineer must establish what the product actually has to achieve. <dfn class="term" data-def="The conditions a product must satisfy in order to be considered a successful solution to the design brief, divided into needs and wants.">Design requirements</dfn> are the full set of conditions the product must satisfy. They come directly from analysing the brief, researching the user, and understanding the context of use. Getting the requirements right is critical: a product that is beautifully made but fails to meet its requirements is a failed product, regardless of its technical quality.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Needs and Wants</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Design requirements are divided into two categories. <dfn class="term" data-def="Essential requirements that a product must meet in order to function correctly and satisfy the brief — non-negotiable criteria.">Needs</dfn> are non-negotiable: they are the essential criteria without which the product simply cannot fulfil its purpose. A bicycle helmet needs to protect the user's head in a collision — that is a need. <dfn class="term" data-def="Desirable features that would improve the product but are not essential to its core function — sometimes called 'nice to haves'.">Wants</dfn> are desirable but optional features that would improve the product without being essential — for example, the same helmet being available in multiple colours. In a specification, needs take priority over wants, and wants are usually only pursued if time and budget allow.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The distinction between needs and wants matters in the exam because you may be asked to classify a list of requirements or to argue why a particular criterion is a need rather than a want. Always justify your classification by referring back to the product's core function and the safety or performance consequences of omitting that feature.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover the box and write from memory: what is the difference between a need and a want? Give an engineering example of each. Then write one sentence explaining why needs take priority over wants in a specification.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Needs are essential — without them the product cannot fulfil its purpose or may be unsafe. Wants are desirable — they improve the product but their absence does not make it non-functional. In a design specification, needs must be satisfied before wants are considered. This hierarchy directly affects decisions about cost, time and scope during the engineering design process.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Quantitative and Qualitative Criteria</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Design specifications contain two types of criteria. <dfn class="term" data-def="Specification criteria expressed as measurable numbers or ranges — for example, a maximum weight of 500 g or a minimum load bearing of 200 N.">Quantitative criteria</dfn> are expressed as numbers or ranges that can be measured objectively — for example, "the product must weigh no more than 500 g" or "the handle must fit hands in the 5th to 95th percentile range." Quantitative criteria make it straightforward to test whether a prototype meets the specification: either the measurement passes or it does not.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Qualitative criteria describe characteristics that cannot easily be reduced to a number — appearance, feel, style, and user satisfaction. For example, "the product must have a professional appearance suitable for an office environment" is qualitative. Qualitative criteria are harder to test objectively and typically require user feedback or expert judgment. A strong specification contains both types: quantitative criteria ensure functional performance; qualitative criteria ensure the product meets user expectations that numbers alone cannot capture.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why both types of criteria matter in a real specification</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Consider a specification for a new children's stacking toy. Quantitative criteria might include: maximum dimension of any piece = 40 mm (choking hazard regulation), minimum compressive strength = 50 N, production cost per unit = under £1.80. Qualitative criteria might include: bright, primary colours that appeal to children aged 2–5; pieces that feel satisfying to click together; a design that parents perceive as durable and safe. Neither type alone is sufficient. A toy that passes all quantitative tests but looks unappealing will not sell; a toy that looks perfect but fails the strength test is unsafe. The combination of quantitative and qualitative criteria in the specification gives the design team clear, complete targets to design and test against.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">The Design Specification and ACCESS FM</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The <dfn class="term" data-def="A detailed document that translates the design brief and research findings into precise, testable criteria organised under standard headings, used to guide and evaluate the design throughout the iterative process.">design specification</dfn> is the document that translates all the requirements — needs and wants, quantitative and qualitative — into a formal set of criteria that the design must meet. The ACCESS FM framework provides the standard structure for organising specification criteria: Aesthetics, Cost, Customer, Environment, Size, Safety, Function, Materials and Manufacturing.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Each heading prompts specific questions. Aesthetics: what must the product look, feel or sound like? Cost: what is the maximum unit production cost? Customer: who will use it and what are their needs? Environment: will it be used indoors or outdoors; are there temperature, moisture or corrosion constraints? Size: what are the maximum and minimum dimensions? Safety: what hazards must be mitigated; which regulations apply? Function: what must the product do and how well? Materials: what material properties are required (strength, flexibility, food-safe, etc.)? Manufacturing: how will it be made; in what quantities; are there process constraints?</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Practise writing a two-line specification criterion under each ACCESS FM heading for a simple product, such as a reusable water bottle. Include at least one quantitative and one qualitative criterion.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>ACCESS FM is used at two points in the iterative process: first, as a framework for analysing existing products during secondary research (covered in Lesson 2); and second, as the structure for writing the design specification. Knowing that the same nine headings serve both purposes — and being able to apply them in both contexts — is a frequently examined point.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Using the Specification Throughout the Design Process</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">A design specification is not written once and forgotten. In the iterative process, the specification is a live reference document: every design idea is checked against it, every model is assessed against it, and every prototype is evaluated against it. When a prototype fails to meet a criterion, the designer knows exactly what to change. When all criteria are met, the designer has evidence that the product is a successful solution to the brief. This is why writing a precise specification — with measurable criteria where possible — is so important: vague criteria produce vague evaluation and no clear basis for decision-making.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common mistake: writing vague specification criteria</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Weak specification criteria are a common source of lost marks. "The product must be strong" is too vague to test — replace it with "the product must withstand a compressive force of at least 150 N without permanent deformation." "The product must look nice" cannot be evaluated — replace it with "the product must have a finish that users in a target focus group rate as aesthetically appropriate for a professional office environment." In exam questions that ask you to write or improve specification criteria, always aim for precision: include a number or a testable condition wherever possible, and name the ACCESS FM heading the criterion belongs under.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Design requirements are divided into needs (essential, non-negotiable) and wants (desirable but optional), and into quantitative criteria (measurable numbers) and qualitative criteria (descriptive characteristics that require user judgment to evaluate).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The design specification translates all requirements into precise, testable criteria organised under the nine ACCESS FM headings — Aesthetics, Cost, Customer, Environment, Size, Safety, Function, Materials and Manufacturing.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The specification is used throughout the entire iterative process to guide design decisions and to evaluate models and prototypes — a precise specification with quantitative criteria enables clear, evidence-based evaluation.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">When asked to write specification criteria in an exam, use ACCESS FM headings to organise your answer and make at least two of your criteria quantitative (include a number, measurement or range). Always distinguish needs from wants if the question asks you to prioritise — state what the product must do (need) before what it could do (want). A common error is to write the same type of criterion repeatedly under different headings; aim for variety across Aesthetics, Function, Size, Safety and Cost at a minimum.</p>

