 {
  "text": "Explain one advantage of using a ranking matrix compared to a qualitative comparison when evaluating design ideas.",
  "type": "3 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Advantage (1 mark): a ranking matrix provides numerical scores for each design concept against weighted criteria, allowing objective direct comparison of multiple options. Development (1 mark): unlike qualitative comparison, which relies on descriptive judgements that can be influenced by personal preference, the ranking matrix forces the evaluator to apply consistent scoring to every criterion for every design. Further development (1 mark): for example, if three concepts score 84, 71 and 63 weighted points respectively, the designer has a clear, defensible basis for selecting concept one — rather than a subjective statement that it 'looks better'. [3 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Describe how a ranking matrix is constructed and used to evaluate design ideas. Include reference to weighted criteria.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Description (up to 2 marks): criteria from the design specification are listed in rows and each design concept is given a column; each criterion is assigned a weighting (e.g. 1–5) reflecting its importance, and each design is scored against each criterion (e.g. 1–5); the score is multiplied by the weighting and totals are summed for each design concept (2 marks for a clear developed description including both scoring and weighting; 1 mark for a basic statement). Application (up to 2 marks): for example, if safety has a weighting of 5 and a design scores 4 for safety, its weighted score for that criterion is 20; this means safety contributes far more to the total than a criterion weighted 1, ensuring the most important requirements drive the selection decision (2 marks for a specific applied example; 1 mark for a vague point about importance). [4 marks total]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A design team has produced three concept designs for a new children's ride-on toy. They need to select the best concept to develop further before prototyping.\n\nDiscuss which evaluation method(s) would be most appropriate to help the design team select the best concept, and explain the reasons for your choice.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): A balanced discussion that selects and justifies at least two evaluation methods with reasoning applied to the scenario. A ranking matrix is well-suited because it allows all three concepts to be scored against weighted specification criteria (such as safety, cost, aesthetics and ease of manufacture) simultaneously, providing a numerical basis for selection — important for a children's product where safety must be heavily weighted. A qualitative comparison with the brief could complement this by providing written justification for scores, particularly for subjective criteria such as aesthetic appeal. The discussion addresses both the strengths and any limitations of the chosen methods in the context of selecting between three concepts.\nSecure (3–4 marks): Two evaluation methods correctly identified with some justification but limited development or scenario application.\nDeveloping (2 marks): One method identified with a reason.\nEmerging (1 mark): A single relevant point with no development. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A product design team is using QFD (Quality Function Deployment) to evaluate a new design for a portable electric drill. The customer requirements include: lightweight, comfortable grip, powerful, long battery life and affordable.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which QFD is a useful tool for evaluating competing design concepts at this stage of the design process.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation identifying the genuine strengths of QFD in this context — it maps all five customer requirements onto engineering characteristics (motor power, battery capacity, housing weight, grip geometry, component cost), reveals the strength of each relationship, and — critically — the roof section exposes trade-offs such as the conflict between lightweight and high power (a larger, heavier motor provides more power). This is especially valuable for a complex multi-criteria product like a drill. Then genuinely challenged with counterpoints: QFD is complex to construct correctly and time-consuming; for early concept comparison it may be more effort than a ranking matrix; the quality of the output depends on correctly identifying the engineering characteristics; and it is not specifically designed to compare discrete design concepts against each other (a ranking matrix does this better). A clear judgement is reached — for example, that QFD is most valuable for understanding relationships and trade-offs between requirements rather than for ranking specific concepts, and that a ranking matrix may be more appropriate for the specific task of selecting between competing concepts.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Strengths and limitations identified with some development and an attempt at a judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Largely one-sided with limited counterargument.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic description of QFD with no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
]

