=== L10 Modelling Methods & Evaluating a Design Outcome ===
id: a3e05e16-7b26-4633-bae6-ed571c868c86
slug: lesson-10
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:44:22.679624+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Modelling methods — virtual 3D CAD, card, block, breadboarding and 3D printing — plus methods of evaluating the.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Model a Design?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A representation of a design idea built to test specific properties — such as proportions, scale, function or appearance — before committing to full-scale manufacture.">model</dfn> allows a designer to test and refine a design idea at relatively low cost before committing to expensive tooling or full manufacture. Models can test proportions (is the product the right size visually?), scale (does it fit in the intended space?), and function (do the moving parts work correctly? does the electronic circuit perform as designed?). The type of model chosen depends on what needs to be tested: a rough card model is made in minutes and reveals shape; a 3D-printed prototype takes hours but reveals surface quality and assembly fit; a virtual 3D CAD model takes longer to build but can be tested digitally for strength and interference before any material is used.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Virtual 3D CAD Modelling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Virtual modelling uses <dfn class="term" data-def="A 3D CAD model built digitally, from which properties such as mass, centre of gravity, stress distribution and assembly clearance can be analysed without producing any physical object.">3D CAD modelling software</dfn> to build a digital representation of the design. Equipment required: a computer with 3D parametric CAD software (such as SolidWorks, Fusion 360 or Creo). Stages: create 2D sketches of cross-sections, extrude or revolve them to form 3D solids, add features (holes, fillets, chamfers), apply materials and simulate loading. The information obtained includes: visual appearance and proportions, mass and centre of gravity, stress concentration under load (FEA), assembly clearances, and interference between parts. Advantage: no material is consumed; changes are instant. Limitation: cannot test real-world surface feel, assembly by hand, or robustness under actual use conditions.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall all five modelling methods from the specification: virtual 3D CAD, card, block, breadboarding, 3D printing. For each, state the main type of information it provides.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five modelling methods in the specification are: virtual 3D CAD, card, block, breadboarding and 3D printing. Each provides different information: CAD gives digital analysis; card and block give proportions and scale; breadboarding tests electronic function; 3D printing produces a near-prototype-quality physical model.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Card Modelling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Card modelling involves cutting, folding and gluing sheets of card (or foam board) to produce a quick, low-cost physical representation of a design. Equipment required: card or foam board, scalpel or scissors, steel rule, cutting mat, adhesive. Stages: draw out the net of the form on card, cut and score fold lines, fold and glue to produce the 3D shape, add detail features with additional pieces. The information obtained is primarily proportions and visual scale — the model shows whether the product is the right size in three dimensions and can be placed in its intended context (e.g. on a desk or in a user's hand). Advantage: very fast and cheap to produce; no specialist skills required. Limitation: cannot test function, structural strength or surface finish; card has very different material properties to the final product.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Block Modelling</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A physical model carved or machined from a solid block of material — typically foam, balsa wood, clay or MDF — to produce a representation of the product's external form and ergonomics.">block model</dfn> is carved or shaped from a solid block of material — commonly modelling foam (such as polyurethane foam), balsa wood, clay or MDF. Equipment required: the block material, cutting tools (craft knife, rasp, sandpaper), and finishing materials (filler, paint). Stages: rough out the overall shape, refine the form with rasps and sandpaper, apply filler and primer, paint to represent the final appearance. The information obtained includes external form, ergonomics (how the product feels in the hand), surface curves and aesthetic quality. Block models are used to test the ergonomic feel of products such as hand tools, telephones and medical devices. Advantage: produces a realistic model of the final form. Limitation: time-consuming to produce; cannot test internal mechanisms or function.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Breadboarding and 3D Printing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A breadboard is a reusable plastic board with a grid of holes connected by metal strips that allow electronic components to be inserted and wired without soldering. Equipment required: breadboard, electronic components (resistors, LEDs, ICs, etc.), jumper wires, power supply. Stages: place components in the appropriate rows, connect them with jumper wires according to the circuit diagram, power the circuit and test its function. The information obtained is whether the electronic circuit works as designed — component values, switching behaviour and signal levels can all be tested and adjusted rapidly. Advantage: components can be reused; the circuit can be modified without desoldering. Limitation: not suitable for high-frequency circuits; connections are less secure than soldered joints; layout bears no resemblance to the final PCB.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">3D printing (additive manufacture) builds a physical object layer by layer from a 3D CAD file (typically STL format), using materials such as PLA or ABS plastic. Equipment required: a 3D printer, CAD software to produce the STL file, and slicing software to generate the print toolpaths. Stages: produce the 3D CAD model, export as STL, slice the file to generate G-code, load material filament, print layer by layer, remove support material and finish the surface. The information obtained includes: dimensional accuracy, surface quality, assembly fit between parts, weight, and visual appearance very close to the final product. Advantage: high geometric complexity is possible; much faster than traditional machining for one-off models; directly linked to the CAD model so dimensional accuracy is high. Limitation: layer-by-layer construction can leave visible surface ridges; material properties (strength, flexibility) of PLA/ABS differ from production materials; large parts require long print times.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Evaluating a Design Outcome</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Once a prototype or final product has been produced, the design must be evaluated as an outcome — not just as an idea. <dfn class="term" data-def="Comparison of the manufactured product against the numerical criteria in the design brief and specification, using measurements to determine whether each quantitative requirement has been met.">Quantitative comparison</dfn> involves measuring the product against numerical criteria in the specification: dimensions are measured using vernier callipers, micrometers, steel rules or CMM (coordinate measuring machine); electrical performance is measured with a multimeter or oscilloscope; mass is measured on a balance. Each measured value is compared against the specification tolerance — pass or fail is determined objectively.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Distinguish clearly between evaluating design IDEAS (ranking matrix, QFD, qualitative comparison, models) and evaluating the design OUTCOME (quantitative comparison, user testing, measuring dimensions and functionality).">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Methods of evaluating a design outcome include: measuring dimensions and functionality (quantitative comparison against the specification), user testing, and identifying potential modifications and improvements. These differ from methods of evaluating design ideas — they are applied after manufacture, not before.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">User Testing and Identifying Modifications</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">User testing involves giving the product to representative users and observing how they interact with it, or gathering structured feedback through questionnaires, interviews or observation checklists. User testing reveals ergonomic issues, misunderstandings in the interface, assembly difficulties and safety concerns that cannot be found through dimensional measurement alone. The feedback is compared with the qualitative criteria in the specification — for example, whether the product is perceived as comfortable, attractive or easy to use. Following evaluation, the designer identifies potential modifications and improvements: features that fail a quantitative check must be redesigned to bring them within tolerance; features that fail user testing may need form changes, material changes or instruction redesign. Identifying modifications and improvements is not a failure — it is the expected output of a properly conducted evaluation and drives the next iteration of the design.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Comparing Modelling Methods — Exam Summary</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">For comparison questions, know each method's key information, equipment, stages, advantage and limitation. Virtual 3D CAD: digital, no material used, tests mass/stress/clearance; limitation — no physical feel. Card: cheap and fast, tests proportions and scale; limitation — no function or strength testing. Block: tests external form and ergonomics; limitation — slow to produce, no function. Breadboarding: tests electronic circuit function; limitation — not suitable for high-frequency signals. 3D printing: near-prototype quality, high complexity, directly from CAD; limitation — layer ridges, material properties differ from production. Always select the modelling method that matches what the designer needs to find out — this is a common exam question structure.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">The five modelling methods in the specification — virtual 3D CAD, card, block, breadboarding and 3D printing — each provide different information: CAD gives digital analysis of mass, stress and clearance; card and block test proportions and ergonomics; breadboarding tests electronic circuit function; 3D printing produces a near-prototype-quality physical model directly from a CAD file.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Evaluating a design outcome involves quantitative comparison (measuring dimensions and functionality against specification tolerances) and user testing (gathering feedback on ergonomics, usability and appearance from representative users).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Identifying potential modifications and improvements is the expected outcome of evaluation and drives the next iteration of the design — it demonstrates that the iterative design process is working correctly.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">Exam questions on modelling often ask you to justify the most appropriate method for a given design scenario. Always anchor your answer to what information the designer needs: if they need to test an electronic circuit, breadboarding is correct; if they need to test ergonomics and external form, a block model is correct; if they need dimensional accuracy close to the final product, 3D printing is correct. Name the method, state what information it provides, and give one limitation. Do not confuse evaluating design ideas (using models, matrices, QFD) with evaluating the design outcome (measuring against the specification after manufacture).</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
